    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-149564",
      "knowledge_point_id": "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing Covariance in Context",
      "problem": "| **Number of Members ($x$)** | $3$ | $4$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Number of Cars ($y$)** | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n\nIn a survey of $4$ families, the number of members in each family was recorded as well as the number of cars owned by that family. The data is shown in the table above.\nCompute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=4$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=3.$",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(3-4)(2-3)+(4-4)(2-3)+(4-4)(2-3)+(5-4)(6-3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(-1)+(0)(-1)+(0)(-1)+(1)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[1+0+0+3] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
