    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-149618",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing Covariance",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={-12,\\,-10,\\,-9,\\,-13},\\,y={-2,\\,0,\\,3,\\,3}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-12-10-9-13) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-44) \\\\  & =-11 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-2+0+3+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-12-(-11))(-2-1)+(-10-(-11))(0-1) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,+(-9-(-11))(3-1)+(-13-(-11))(3-1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(-3)+(1)(-1)+(2)(2)+(-2)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[3-1+4-4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
